it may be autumn but Cordova is in full  bloom eight top international florists  have let their imaginations run wild for  displays inside public courtyards across  the southern Spanish city flora is both  a competition for the artists and a  festival for the public this courtyard  features it work by Tomas Sabrina of  Belgium the internationally acclaimed  floral artist brought some 2000 as a  leas to Cordova I was inspired by the  Paradise in Cordoba so first of all I  used the possio possio it's paradise for  Cordoba and second I also used like a  filigrana Cordoba  that's the craftsmanship people really  loved it accord over so those two  together I want to have a dialogue with  the patio with the craftsmanship I wait  the festival to create a dialogue with  the people one day before the festival  opened he was still busy with 22  assistants who had just four days to  transform his vision into a floral  reality every detail matters  Tomas Sabrina is demanding we have to  redo it out yes out redo we need  perfection the edge between the plants  in the wall is like almost like three  centimeter four centimeters too wide so  they have to move all the plants three  four centimeters to the wall that like  everything is suits bell it's part of  the concept  a Chinese entrepreneur donated a total  of 120 thousand euros in prize money  awards are given to the three best  entries also in the running is Robert  Hornsby the creative director of  London's in-water flowers his works  provide food or flowers for thought  [Music]  reflections is basically looking at  yourself looking at your soul looking at  who you are where you came from what are  you doing are you happy I think when  people see themselves in the mirror it's  very challenging sometimes the ultimate  question is are you happy today am I  happy today  do I like myself the courtyard of the  Archaeological Museum is the backdrop  for an installation by Patrick Nadeau  from France a trained architect with a  passion for plants he counts companies  like MS and Louie Vuitton as his  customers he's particularly interested  in archaeology archaeology is the  history of civilization it's the history  of humankind of things or architecture  and all of that could only arise because  before there were humans there were  plants they're the source of life that  give us the air we breathe that's why I  find it so compelling to install a kind  of enormous vessel filled with plants at  the heart of an archeological Museum  [Music]  news about my yes is from Madrid she  used to work as a fashion designer but  for the last seven years she's been  designing floral artworks like this one  in the inner courtyard of Cordova's  bullfighting Museum  [Music]  this is an extraterrestrial garden from  another planet to get away from  traditional ideas of what a paradise  should look like  I didn't use any green plants and only  chose ones with unusual colors in this  way I hope to create a kind of dream  world  an imaginary world a fantasy ax Cordoba  is famous for its inner courtyards known  as patios hardly any other city in Spain  boasts as many on behalf of the city  Emilia Ruiz Mateo organized the  International Flower Festival flora and  brought the floral artists to Andalusia  patios of Cordoba play a central role  here they date from Roman times were  perfected by the Arabs and are just the  place for flowers that's what we wanted  to link this ancient culture with a  passion for flowers modern floral  creations surrounded by historical  architecture the prize for the loveliest  patio went to this installation designed  by two Americans the jury awarded Tomas  de Bruyne as patios second place and  40,000 euros in prize money the Belgian  floral artist has already jetted off to  his next assignment in San Francisco but  visitors can still admire his floral  paradise in Cordoba until the end of  October  [Music]  you
 

